{"step_index":104,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-15T08:58:31Z","model":"gemini-3.8-flash-high","input_tokens":3944,"cache_read_tokens":101863,"output_tokens":1843,"thinking":"The successful gzip encoding suggests a potential bandwidth bottleneck reduction. Considering user feedback about initial lag, the investigation shifts to potential causes of slow first-load rendering. Initial hypothesis centers on blocking resources within the `<head>` of the HTML, specifically the Google Fonts loading.\n\n\nExternal font loading from multiple domains (`googleapis.com` and `gstatic.com`) at the beginning of the page render could introduce latency, especially with slower connections. Synchronous Google Tag Manager (GTM) and Google Analytics (GA) scripts included in the `<head>` are likely blocking initial execution and contributing to performance degradation. The asynchronous attribute does not prevent the initial blocking behavior, but it will enable the browser to keep parsing other resources.\n\n\nImmediate blocking script loading in the `<head>` is confirmed; a significant offender is `backs.min.js`. Further analysis reveals a 212KB AI chatbot script (`kezza-ai.min.js`) loads unnecessarily on initial page load, consuming substantial resources, and contributing to lag. Several scripts, including `scroll-3d.js`, `smooth-scroll.js`, and `pro-animations.js`, attach event listeners and perform DOM queries immediately on startup, further compounding the performance issue.\n\n\nMultiple scripts execute immediately on page load, attaching event listeners, querying the DOM extensively, and running animations, indicating a potential performance bottleneck. The high CPU/GPU load stems from concurrent animations, including the hero slideshow and infinite marquee scrolls, which are initialized simultaneously upon page load, creating significant resource competition. Loading several large WebP images for the hero slideshow in a continuous 800% sliding container is likely a major contributor to resource usage.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":60,"Path":"/Users/ravi/Desktop/kezza_clinic-main/frontend/face-scanner.html","StartLine":1}}]}
